messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain. I think the correct option is: A\n', 'To solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given context and analyze the statement step by step.\n\n### Context Breakdown:.\n1. **If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.**.\n   - \\( S \\rightarrow M \\) (S = Student Jobs, M = Money to Pay Tuition).\n\n2. **If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.**.\n   - \\( T \\rightarrow S \\) (T = Order Takeout Frequently).\n\n3. **People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.**.\n   - \\( T \\lor D \\) (T = Order Takeout Frequently, D = Enjoy Dining Hall Meals and Recipes).\n\n4. **If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.**.\n   - \\( D \\rightarrow \\neg P \\) (D = Enjoy Dining Hall Meals and Recipes, P = Picky Eater).\n\n5. **If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - \\( D \\rightarrow E \\) (D = Enjoy Dining Hall Meals and Recipes, E = Spend Time Eating and Catching Up).\n\n6. **If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**.\n   - \\( S \\land M \\rightarrow \\neg P \\land \\neg M \\) (S = Student Jobs, M = Money to Pay Tuition, P = Picky Eater).\n\n### Analyzing the Statement:.\nThe statement to evaluate is:.\n- **Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - \\( \\neg P \\lor (P \\rightarrow E) \\).\n\nLet\'s break this down:.\n- \\( P \\rightarrow E \\) means "If Mary is a picky eater, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.".\n- \\( \\neg P \\lor (P \\rightarrow E) \\) means "Mary is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.".\n\n### Logical Analysis:.\n1. **Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**.\n   - From the context, \\( S \\land M \\rightarrow \\neg P \\land \\neg M \\) implies that if Mary works in student jobs and needs money, she cannot be a picky eater and does not need to earn money. This means \\( \\neg P \\) and \\( \\neg M \\).\n\n2. **If Mary is a picky eater, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - This is directly given in the context: \\( D \\rightarrow E \\).\n\n3. **Mary is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - Since \\( \\neg P \\) is true (Mary is not a picky eater), the statement \\( \\neg P \\lor (P \\rightarrow E) \\) is true.\n\n### Conclusion:.\nThe statement is true because Mary is not a picky eater, and the condition \\( P \\rightarrow E \\) does not apply to her. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 57%|#####6    | 116/204 [00:11<00:04, 19.40it/s]
